            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Bengali for to overturn, then the Bengali for injured."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: জ্বলা",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "জ্বলা — jvôlā — \"to burn; to be alight\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "যদি রাতে বাড়িতে আলো জ্বলে, তাহলে বুঝব যে কেউ আছে। jôdi rāte bāṛite ālo jvôle, tāhôle bujhbô je keu āchhe. If there's a light on in the house at night, I'll know someone is there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past (he/she …): সেই বাতি জ্বলল, sei bāti jvôllô."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "জ্বলা (jvôlā) comes down from Sanskrit jvalati, 'burns brightly' (CDIAL 5306, which lists Bengali jalā, 'to burn'). It is the partner of জ্বালানো: you light a lamp, and it burns."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling what happened."
            }
          ]
        },
